  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n   - We propose to use adaptive function expansion and pruning to iteratively refine the step function representation.\n   - Core idea: Start with a simple representation of the function and incrementally add or remove grid points where errors are detected,\n     while maintaining the integral constraint and box constraints.\n   - Key heuristics: \n     - Expand at regions identified by high correlation values\n     - Prune redundant points to smooth out variations\n     - Prioritize high-density regions to focus optimization\n   - Limitations: This is a heuristic method, not convex, and may converge to local optima.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   - Representation: n-point uniform grid distribution\n   - Objective: min max_k (dx * max_lag |np.correlate(h,1-h,'full')[l]|)\n   - Constraints:\n     0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\n     sum(h) = L/2 exactly with L=dx*n_points=2 (so dx=2/n_points)\n   - Variables: n_points (grid resolution) as well as h-values at each grid point\n   - Solver type: Stochastic gradient-free adaptive search\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   - Validate input parent if available\n   - Initialize hyperparameters and bookkeeping variables:\n     - target_overlap = C_0.3807 (parent value)\n     - base_n = initial grid points (360 if parent exists)\n     - expansion_thres = 0.8 (grid value to trigger expansion)\n     - pruning_thres = 0.8 (grid value to trigger pruning)\n     - max_n_points = 1008 (2.8 times base_n)\n     - dt = time per grid refinement (\u22480.1 secbase)\n   - Perturbation and projection functions:\n     - perturb() = random uniform step + constraining adjustment\n     - project_box_sum() = normalize sum to L/2 using bidirectional clipping\n   - Main loop:\n     - {(step 3a) Setup new grid if initial |step 3b) Perturb and evaluate |step 3c) Search candidates |step 3d) Decide grid expansion/pruning |step 3e) Next grid resolution} \n     - Monitoring for timeout and solution quality\n     - Return best candidate on timeout\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n   - Primary: adaptive grid resolution (from base_n to max_n_points)\n   - Backup: fallback to nearest neighbor interpolation for time-critical steps\n   - Variations: controlled randomness in point selection (sigmoid-based influence factor)\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n   - Approximate steps: base_n * adjustment factor * dt seconds\n   - Gradients: skip value-based stopping (only metric-based)\n   - Fallback: grid interpolation from validated reference solution\n   - Cooling: no explicit temperature schedule (heuristic-based)\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n   - Verify integral exactly to base_n/2 tolerance\n   - Evaluate c5_bound only on highest-resolution grid\n   - Maintain grid point count consistency across resolutions\n   - Check closest grid spacing reference for floating quality",
